// ALG1 Unit 05 Lesson 02 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Factoring Quadratic Expressions
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.FND.8; MLA.A1.QE.3

::ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+5x+6\).
{
=(x+2)(x+3)#Correct. 2 and 3 multiply to 6 and add to 5.
~(x+1)(x+6)#These factors multiply to 6 but add to 7.
~(x-2)(x-3)#These factors give a positive constant but a negative middle term.
~(x+5)(x+6)#These factors multiply to 30, not 6.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-7x+12\).
{
~(x+3)(x+4)#These factors give a positive middle term.
=(x-3)(x-4)#Correct. -3 and -4 multiply to 12 and add to -7.
~(x-2)(x-6)#These factors multiply to 12 but add to -8.
~(x+2)(x+6)#These factors give a positive middle term.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+x-20\).
{
~(x+4)(x+5)#These factors multiply to 20, not -20.
~(x-4)(x-5)#These factors multiply to 20 and add to -9.
=(x+5)(x-4)#Correct. 5 and -4 multiply to -20 and add to 1.
~(x-5)(x+4)#These factors multiply to -20 but add to -1.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(3x^2+12x\).
{
~3(x^2+4x)#This removes a factor of 3 but misses the common factor x.
~x(3x+12)#This removes x but leaves a common factor of 3 inside.
~3x(x+12)#Dividing \(12x\) by \(3x\) gives 4, not 12.
=3x(x+4)#Correct. The greatest common factor is \(3x\).
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3]
If \(f(x)=(x-2)(x+5)\), what are the zeros?
{
~2 and 5#The factor \(x+5\) gives zero at x = -5, not 5.
=2 and -5#Correct. Set each factor equal to 0: \(x-2=0\) and \(x+5=0\).
~-2 and 5#This reverses both signs.
~-2 and -5#The factor \(x-2\) gives zero at x = 2.
}
